WEBVTT

00:00:00.744 --> 00:00:02.914
Semicolons and colons.

00:00:02.914 --> 00:00:05.284
A semicolon works like a period.

00:00:05.284 --> 00:00:07.353
A colon says: here's what I mean.

00:00:08.914 --> 00:00:10.834
Here's how to spot one.

00:00:10.834 --> 00:00:14.039
The choices offer semicolons and colons.

00:00:14.039 --> 00:00:16.626
And the blank sits between two parts of the sentence.

00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:20.847
It's testing the exact job of each punctuation mark.

00:00:21.902 --> 00:00:23.367
Here's the method.

00:00:23.367 --> 00:00:26.482
For a semicolon, test both sides.

00:00:26.482 --> 00:00:28.892
Each one must be a full sentence.

00:00:28.892 --> 00:00:31.717
For a colon, test the left side.

00:00:31.717 --> 00:00:33.842
It must be a full sentence.

00:00:33.842 --> 00:00:35.782
Then check the right side.

00:00:35.782 --> 00:00:39.237
Does it explain, list, or give an example?

00:00:39.237 --> 00:00:41.177
That's a colon's job.

00:00:41.177 --> 00:00:43.434
And never put a colon straight after a verb.

00:00:44.531 --> 00:00:48.496
Rule one: a semicolon works exactly like a period.

00:00:48.496 --> 00:00:53.061
Because everyone left can't stand alone, so the semicolon fails.

00:00:53.061 --> 00:00:54.449
Everyone had left can.

00:00:55.506 --> 00:00:59.466
Rule two: a colon needs a full sentence before it.

00:00:59.466 --> 00:01:03.661
The ingredients are, isn't complete, so the colon fails.

00:01:03.661 --> 00:01:04.498
Just drop it.

00:01:05.576 --> 00:01:11.091
Rule three: after a colon comes an explanation, a list, or an example.

00:01:11.091 --> 00:01:13.734
The cause was clear: the battery overheated.

00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:16.210
Let's try one.

00:01:16.210 --> 00:01:20.545
The left side says: the ship's log revealed the cause of the delay.

00:01:20.545 --> 00:01:22.340
That's a full sentence.

00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:27.070
The right side tells us what the cause was, so it explains the left side.

00:01:27.070 --> 00:01:29.470
A comma alone would be a splice.

00:01:29.470 --> 00:01:32.035
No punctuation would be a run-on.

00:01:32.035 --> 00:01:35.650
Adding and turns the explanation into a separate fact.

00:01:35.650 --> 00:01:39.382
A colon introduces an explanation, so the answer is B.

00:01:40.439 --> 00:01:44.389
Watch out for a colon after a verb, or after such as.

00:01:44.389 --> 00:01:47.309
The left side isn't a complete sentence.

00:01:47.309 --> 00:01:50.609
The kit includes, colon, is wrong.

00:01:50.609 --> 00:01:51.663
No colon needed.

00:01:52.714 --> 00:01:59.374
Here's the whole lesson on one page: how to spot it, the four steps, the rules, and the trap.

00:01:59.374 --> 00:02:02.194
Pause the video now, and copy it into your notes.
